<h1>Meridian — transit wayfinding, applied</h1>
<p class="sub">The metro read isn't a costume. A new-tab page and a transit map do the same
job: orient you inside a network of places you return to. Below is the vocabulary that
earns its place, and the parts I'd leave out.</p>

<h2>A · Station stop — the mark sits on the line</h2>
<p class="note">The bar is a line crossing a circle. That is what a meridian <em>is</em> —
so the transit device and the name are the same idea. This is the one I'd build the
system on: the logo is a stop on the Meridian line, and the UI below uses the same line.</p>
<div class="stage">
  <div class="lockup-stop">
    <div class="rail"></div>
    <img class="mark" src="meridian-mark-d.svg" width="52" height="52" alt="">
    <div class="name">Meridian</div>
    <div class="rail"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>B · Nameplate — platform signage</h2>
<p class="note">Highest-impact, narrowest use: splash, store hero, README banner. Fair
warning — a circle with a bar and a name is close to the TfL roundel, so I kept ours a
solid disc with the mark riding <em>on</em> the plate rather than inside a ring.</p>
<div class="stage">
  <div class="lockup-plate">
    <img class="mark" src="meridian-mark-l.svg" width="40" height="40" alt="">
    <div class="name">Meridian</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>C · Quiet lockup — the everyday default</h2>
<p class="note">Not everything should shout. This is what belongs in the top bar.</p>
<div class="stage">
  <div class="lockup-quiet">
    <img class="mark" src="meridian-mark-d.svg" width="44" height="44" alt="">
    <div class="name">Meridian</div>
